          "archive_member": "episodes/annie-pearl/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " used by a sales organization, the head of sales is not the IT admin, but they are a Teams admin who needs to manage their organization within Calendly. So, the enterprise group really thinks both about the admin, but also sort of the departments and how do we better serve departments. \n\n(00:18:31):\nAnd then lastly, we have a platform team who's really thinking about how do we embed Calendly into the business processes of the organizations that we support and that we provide our product into. And so, that's everything from partnerships and integrations to our APIs.\n\nLenny (00:18:46):\nInteresting. So, it's like problem focused/persona focused. Who are you trying to sell it to?\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:18:54):\nThat's right, that's right. Yeah, trying to sell it to, and then the persona of who's going to be using the functionality. And then, really having those teams hone and own those personas as they're developing functionality within the product.\n\nLenny (00:19:07):\nWhat's your take on OKRs? Do you all use OKRs in some form?\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:19:11):\nYes, we do. We use OKRs both at the company level. So, we have three main OKRs that we're focused on for this year, for example, across the whole company. And then we have department level OKRs, many of which are in support of the company level OKRs, but then there's some additional things that we'll be doing at the department level, for example, that aren't going to show up at the company level. So yeah, we use them both at the company as well as on the product side.\n\nLenny (00:19:34):\nIs there anything you've learned about making OKRs work? People love them. People hate them. \n\n",
          "start_char": 22255,
          "end_char": 23899,
          "start_seconds": 1069,
          "end_seconds": 1174,
          "similarity": 0.5747202738555349
        },
        "S6": {
          "chunk_id": "7428e779-7eb3-4de0-8852-445f53561416",
          "episode_id": "65c17ed2-12f3-47ed-80fa-ad86a5f73524",
          "episode_revision_id": "2e5d0a72-4338-49f4-a807-103db5b90558",
          "title": "Behind the scenes of Calendly’s rapid growth | Annie Pearl (CPO)",
          "guest": "Annie Pearl",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-tUIGpgmsZw",
          "archive_member": "episodes/annie-pearl/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": ".\n\nLenny (00:16:59):\nWhat about in terms of the structure, whatever you can share one level below, how do you structure teams? Is it around outcomes? Is it around features of the product? Is it around type of persona? How do you think about that? \n\nAnnie Pearl (00:17:13):\nYeah, yeah. So, we have a core team who's really responsible for the core end to-end user experience. In many ways, they're both doing feature development and then they're also doing growth work. So, they're thinking about how do we build new features and functionalities to help our core personas, which is typically folks who are in sales, recruiting and customer success. So, anyone in an externally facing role, we're really trying to help them do their jobs better. So, the core team's thinking about features and functionalities to really help our core end user persona. And then growth work to think about the PLG funnel, everything from acquisition, activation, conversion, and retention. So, that's one group. \n\n(00:17:49):\nAnd then, second group is our \"enterprise group.\" And they're really thinking about two different personas. One is the IT admin, the person who needs to make sure that Calendly is secure and that they have all the reporting mechanisms to be able to manage their account and all the tools to manage users and groups at scale. And the second piece of that is also departmental leaders. So as Calendly selling into or being used by a sales organization, the head of sales is not the IT admin, but they are a Teams admin who needs to manage their organization within Calendly. So, the enterprise group really thinks both about the admin, but also sort of the departments and how do we better serve departments. \n\n",
          "start_char": 20829,
          "end_char": 22544,
          "start_seconds": 989,
          "end_seconds": 1069,
          "similarity": 0.5621925247989745
        },
        "S7": {
          "chunk_id": "318c66cf-0b9b-4f76-a610-57db22a8076c",
          "episode_id": "65c17ed2-12f3-47ed-80fa-ad86a5f73524",
          "episode_revision_id": "2e5d0a72-4338-49f4-a807-103db5b90558",
          "title": "Behind the scenes of Calendly’s rapid growth | Annie Pearl (CPO)",
          "guest": "Annie Pearl",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-tUIGpgmsZw",
          "archive_member": "episodes/annie-pearl/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " schedule together is growing much faster than our solo user business. And that's really where the future of where we think growth will come from is supporting these teams of users who are in externally facing roles and selling into departments and supporting multi-departmental deployments of Calendly across an entire organization. \n\n(00:44:17):\nSo, I think it's still really well known as this solo user tool to eliminate the back and forth of email, but the growth of what we're seeing and where we think it's going to go is actually more teams of users and departments of users and then multiple departments in an organization.\n\nLenny (00:44:32):\nIt's interesting when you hear the story of Calendly that just has so many good things happening, basically for free, it's just grows so well. I think people don't realize you eventually will, that'll slow down, it'll taper off eventually. You'll need to drive growth very actively in these new ways that you're describing. And I think people don't often realize that they just wanted to find something that was viral and then things are going to go great, but it tapers off.\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:44:54):\nYeah. I mean, there's only so many people who, solo users who are going to pull out a credit card. And I think once you also get to hundreds of millions of dollars of revenue scale, just the law of large numbers, it means that growth will slow. And so, you have to figure out where's that next growth curve going to come from. I think the beauty of Calendly is that while we certainly have built features and functionality to support teams and departments, we got pulled there. It wasn't one of those things where we sort of said, \"We need to find our next growth lever. Let's go build X.\" Our customers really pulled us there by the way that they were using the product. \n\n",
          "start_char": 52607,
          "end_char": 54434,
          "start_seconds": 2623,
          "end_seconds": 2694,
          "similarity": 0.6021707879838848
        },
        "S8": {
          "chunk_id": "3d35f431-33b8-4db6-9210-b4f2fa40b3e8",
          "episode_id": "65c17ed2-12f3-47ed-80fa-ad86a5f73524",
          "episode_revision_id": "2e5d0a72-4338-49f4-a807-103db5b90558",
          "title": "Behind the scenes of Calendly’s rapid growth | Annie Pearl (CPO)",
          "guest": "Annie Pearl",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-tUIGpgmsZw",
          "archive_member": "episodes/annie-pearl/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " features and functionality to support teams and departments, we got pulled there. It wasn't one of those things where we sort of said, \"We need to find our next growth lever. Let's go build X.\" Our customers really pulled us there by the way that they were using the product. \n\n(00:45:30):\nAnd so again, a very fortunate position to be in, but when you can see in the data and see how customers are using it, that they want to be working on scheduling with their teams, that was our early sign, that's where the business was going to go in the future.\n\nLenny (00:45:46):\nI don't think I mentioned this, I'm paying user of Calendly. It's what I use for booking these podcast episodes.\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:45:50):\nAll right.\n\nLenny (00:45:50):\nYou got me. I think I started when it was totally free and I was like, \"How will they ever make money?\" This is too much power. \n\nAnnie Pearl (00:45:56):\nAnd then, now, you learned that it was free almost by accident.\n\nLenny (00:46:02):\nYep. I was like, \"Yeah, please take my money. This makes my life easier.\" What are some fun or unique traditions and cultural kind of components of the Calendly product team?\n\n",
          "start_char": 54155,
          "end_char": 55308,
          "start_seconds": 2694,
          "end_seconds": 2762,
          "similarity": 0.5966719374601266
        },
        "S9": {
          "chunk_id": "244ad1ec-b497-413f-8e16-cd1b945a5724",
          "episode_id": "65c17ed2-12f3-47ed-80fa-ad86a5f73524",
          "episode_revision_id": "2e5d0a72-4338-49f4-a807-103db5b90558",
          "title": "Behind the scenes of Calendly’s rapid growth | Annie Pearl (CPO)",
          "guest": "Annie Pearl",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-tUIGpgmsZw",
          "archive_member": "episodes/annie-pearl/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": "kraine.\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:43:08):\nOh nice. That's awesome.\n\nLenny (00:43:08):\nThat's pretty cool.\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:43:09):\nYeah, they're great. [inaudible 00:43:11].\n\nLenny (00:43:11):\nYeah, and it's also interesting that it's rare that you hear a successful business starts with contractor engineers. I think OICs are like, \"Do not do that.\" So that's a cool counter example of it can actually work out, especially if they're your first users and spread it to others. \n\nAnnie Pearl (00:43:27):\nAnd we still work with them. They're fantastic and they have incredible engineers, so they're still part of our culture, which is great.\n\nLenny (00:43:32):\nSo, Calendly got big in Ukraine, it sounds like initially.\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:43:35):\nThere you go. There you go.\n\nLenny (00:43:37):\nWhat's something that would surprise people in terms of how Calendly grows today or grew through its history?\n\nAnnie Pearl (00:43:43):\nMost people probably think about Calendly as the scheduling link and really for individual users to reduce the back and forth of email and scheduling. So, they think of that one-on-one use case and I think people would be surprised to learn that our team's business, so multiple users in an organization who want to collaboratively schedule together is growing much faster than our solo user business. And that's really where the future of where we think growth will come from is supporting these teams of users who are in externally facing roles and selling into departments and supporting multi-departmental deployments of Calendly across an entire organization. \n\n",
          "start_char": 51360,
          "end_char": 52943,
          "start_seconds": 2583,
          "end_seconds": 2623,
          "similarity": 0.6239853867277514
        },
        "S10": {
          "chunk_id": "6d5a0dd0-91a0-4323-850b-2cb6f8b5c73e",
          "episode_id": "18f8e329-0199-4dfa-91d9-8166db484b43",
          "episode_revision_id": "0bfe275e-1ec5-4384-bd4d-55d0fd6d53b7",
          "title": "How to achieve hypergrowth in your business and career | Carilu Dietrich (Atlassian)",
          "guest": "Carilu Dietrich",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pm7QSWDIEUc",
          "archive_member": "episodes/carilu-dietrich/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " with companies, because almost no one can do pure product led growth. Because your investors want to see accelerated growth, and most people believe that adding an SDR or adding an AE will add a predictable amount of revenue. And so it's difficult because product led growth and sales have to coexist.\n\n(00:39:16):\nBut the whole transition in marketing that's happening to account-based marketing and intent-based signals is trying to do some of what we were doing at Atlassian, only to engage salespeople with customers that are actually likely to buy. And if you look at other hyper-growth companies, Airtable, Miro, both of those I know from the inside have had strategies where the salespeople really only engage after some threshold has been hit of number of users that have already been paying for the product on their credit card. But sometimes it's a high number of 20, 40 people at an account have to be engaged before a salesperson engages.\n\nLenny (00:39:54):\nBased on that, what is it you recommend to founders these days that maybe are product led and just self-service [inaudible 00:40:00] oriented. Do you kind of take this experience and wait as long as you can to hire your first salesperson? I know it's very specific to the situation, but what kind of advice do you generally give?\n\n",
          "start_char": 39730,
          "end_char": 41032,
          "start_seconds": 2331,
          "end_seconds": 2394,
          "similarity": 0.571456082264999
        },
        "S11": {
          "chunk_id": "99c7ab0a-7e56-4069-8996-606cd188e2cb",
          "episode_id": "4b8cf594-10bf-4344-9b6e-df450a8995d9",
          "episode_revision_id": "24e5b24e-4120-49b3-af59-4105b97fcc23",
          "title": "Founder-led sales | Pete Kazanjy (Founding Sales, Atrium)",
          "guest": "Pete Kazanjy",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cZd5234Eem0",
          "archive_member": "episodes/pete-kazanjy/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " for sales motions, and so it's in your brain, it's in your documents, et cetera, and so now, you're going to teach these other folks. So, what you want to do is you want to hire a couple early-stage pioneer sellers to take that sales motion.\n\n(00:41:30):\nThe downside of seeking to hire a VP of sales or a head of sales, or what have you, who actually is a head of sales is coming out of an organization where maybe he or she is a manager of managers or manages eight reps or something like that, is that person hasn't been selling for a hot second. I think actually Jason Lemkin had a pretty funny tweet about this the other day where he was like, \"Hiring the VP of sales who's been there and done that before, why exactly does she want to do it again?\" Like, \"Oh, you scaled up a Datadog or Figma or whatever, you should come to my crappy little startup,\" it's like, \"I'm professionally rich.\" So, instead, the great folks to look at are the deputies or those early-stage sellers.\n\n(00:42:18):\nSo, the example I always use here, my last software company was in recruiting, so if you have a new recruiting technology, my buddy, Troy, runs this recruiting software company called Guide. They make this really cool, guided, hey, hiring process for candidates, whatever, but they sell to recruiting organizations. So the early-stage sellers that he would be interested in would probably be early people at Lever or Greenhouse who sell to the same persona, probably around the same average selling price, but ideally, not an AE who joined Greenhouse last year or two years ago.\n\n",
          "start_char": 43254,
          "end_char": 44831,
          "start_seconds": 2433,
          "end_seconds": 2538,
          "similarity": 0.5493607442332034
        }
      },
      "used_context_tokens": 3987,
      "context_hit": true,
      "answer_seconds": 7.577461999841034,
      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Calendly added sales on top of a business that was almost entirely PLG, initially using inbound interest and product-qualified signals to approach teams already using the product. That early motion suited reps with relevant inbound or expansion-selling experience, rather than outbound-heavy hunters.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2",
          "S3"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "How Calendly began the sales-led motion",
            "content": "When Annie Pearl joined, Calendly had just hired its first CRO; PLG accounted for 99% of ARR and there was not yet a sales team. As the team scaled the sales-led motion, it largely started with inbound leads and product-qualified leads (PQLs): usage data showed that people on a team were using Calendly, so sales could contact the team lead about expanding it across the organization. Typically, solo users invited teammates, and the team’s leader either contacted Calendly or was identified through PQL data. Over the following two years, sales-led growth reached about 20% of ARR and was the fastest-growing segment.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S3",
              "S4"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Reps suited to the early approach",
            "content": "The early motion was more inbound and focused on growing usage within teams than on heavy outbound prospecting. Pearl said the best fit was therefore not necessarily an outbound-heavy hunter; a grower profile was more appropriate. For hiring, she emphasized candidates’ backgrounds and the kind of selling they had previously done more than personality type. The initial buyer was generally a department head—such as a head of sales, revenue operations, or recruiting—not a senior IT buyer or CIO.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S2"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": []
      },
