        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Calendly added sales to its PLG business by following team usage signals and engaging interested leads to expand within organizations. That called for reps suited to inbound, growth-oriented selling—not primarily outbound hunters.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2",
          "S3"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "How the sales-led motion began",
            "content": "When Annie Pearl joined, Calendly had just hired its first CRO and PLG accounted for 99% of ARR. Over the next two years, the company built out sales; the sales-led motion reached about 20% of ARR and was its fastest-growing segment. The motion followed product use: solo users invited teammates, and when a team was active, its lead might inbound or Calendly would use product-qualified-lead (PQL) data to reach out and discuss expanding Calendly across the organization. About 70% of signups came through the viral loop Pearl described.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S3"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Reps suited to the early approach",
            "content": "Because the initial motion was mostly inbound—people who had reached out or leads whose team usage signaled interest—Calendly needed reps more oriented toward growing accounts than hunting for prospects through heavy outbound. Pearl said prior background and the type of selling a candidate had done mattered more than personality. The typical buyer was a department head, such as a head of sales, revenue operations, or recruiting, rather than a senior IT leader or CIO.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
